            0.432,
            0.158
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "13.4 Median of Grouped Data"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.165,
            0.902,
            0.334
          ],
          "content": "As you have studied in Class IX, the median is a measure of central tendency which gives the value of the middle-most observation in the data. Recall that for finding the median of ungrouped data, we first arrange the data values of the observations in ascending order. Then, if n is odd, the median is the $\\left( { \\frac { n + 1 } { 2 } } \\right)$ th observation. And, if n is even, then the median will be the average of the $\\frac { n } { 2 }$ thand the $\\left( { \\frac { n } { 2 } } + 1 \\right)$ th observations."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.351,
            0.9,
            0.389
          ],
          "content": "Suppose, we have to find the median of the following data, which gives the marks, out of 50, obtained by 100 students in a test :"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "bbox": [
            0.125,
            0.396,
            0.862,
            0.481
          ],
          "content": "| Marks obtained | 20 | 29 | 28 | 33 | 42 | 38 | 43 | 25 |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Number of students | 6 | 28 | 24 | 15 | 2 | 4 | 1 | 20 |",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_17_table_5.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.489,
            0.899,
            0.527
          ],
          "content": "First, we arrange the marks in ascending order and prepare a frequency table as follows :"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "bbox": [
            0.228,
            0.557,
            0.812,
            0.893
          ],
